EXT. BRIAR FOREST CROSSROADS - DUSK

Four roads meet at a mossy standing stone. Branches arch overhead, thick with fireflies. A stag skull is nailed to a signpost, antlers hung with ribbons.

A HOODED FIGURE sits on the stone, legs crossed, cloak the color of old moss. Hands rest on knees. Waiting.

From the north road, a mule-drawn cart creaks into view. GARRICK, 40s, wiry cartwright, perches on the driver's board, a long whip coiled in his lap. Walking beside him, YSOLDE, 20s, a student of herbs in a patched green cloak, carries a basket under her arm.

They stop at the stone. Garrick tips his hat. The Hooded Figure doesn't acknowledge it.

GARRICK
Evening.

HOODED FIGURE
Evening.

YSOLDE
Is this the road to Marrow Hill?

HOODED FIGURE
All four roads lead to Marrow Hill. Only one arrives.

GARRICK
Cryptic sort, aren't you?

HOODED FIGURE
I am old.

YSOLDE
Which one arrives?

The Hooded Figure lifts a hand and points, without looking, along the east road, then the west, then the south.

HOODED FIGURE
Not that one. Not that one. Not that one.

GARRICK
That leaves the north.

HOODED FIGURE
It does.

GARRICK
We came from the north.

HOODED FIGURE
Then you have already arrived.

Ysolde and Garrick exchange a look. A rustle in the brush behind the stone. PELLEGRIN, 60s, a tall monk in a threadbare brown habit, steps into the road with a staff in each hand. He stops, taking in the cart, the girl, the stone.

PELLEGRIN
Ah. Company.

GARRICK
Father.

PELLEGRIN
Not Father. Just Pellegrin.

YSOLDE
Do you know the way?

PELLEGRIN
No. But I know when to stop.

He plants both staffs and leans on them. The Hooded Figure rises from the stone. Pulls back the hood. A woman, lined face, eyes bright as a bird's.

MAREN, 70s, stands at the crossroads with her hands open.

MAREN
I am Maren. I've kept this crossing for sixty years.

GARRICK
For what?

MAREN
For those who are lost enough to ask directions.

PELLEGRIN
And are we lost?

MAREN
Obviously.

She sits again. Opens a satchel at her side and takes out a loaf of bread. Tears it in four. Offers the pieces to each.

YSOLDE
Thank you.

GARRICK
What's the catch?

MAREN
Eat. Then we'll see.

They eat. The fireflies drift. The stag skull watches, and the four roads stretch into the dark, patient as old dogs.
